    "case_id": "514",
    "case_summary": "A 59-year-old man with a history of hypertension and medication-induced diabetes presented with new-onset heart failure symptoms. The symptoms began 2 weeks after receiving a COVID-19 vaccination booster. The patient had no significant family history. Past medical conditions included hypertension and medication-induced diabetes.\n\nClinical examination revealed signs of volume overload with presence of lower-extremity edema and jugular venous distention.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Blood urea nitrogen: 46 mg/dL    * Creatinine: 1.79 mg/dL (estimated glomerular filtration rate: 43 mL/min/1.73 m²)    * Uric acid: 14.1 mg/dL    * N-terminal pro B-type natriuretic peptide: 206 ng/dL    * Negative results for tuberculin skin testing, rheumatoid factor, antinuclear antibody, and viral studies- Imaging Studies:    * Electrocardiogram: PR depressions and inferolateral ST-segment elevation    * Echocardiogram:      - Left ventricular ejection fraction: 64%      - Right ventricular dysfunction (tricuspid annular plane systolic excursion: 15 mm)      - Increased inferior vena cava size: 28 mm with reduced inspiratory collapse (<50%)      - Mild tricuspid regurgitation      Image Title: Figure 1      Image Description: Echocardiograph showing reduced inspiratory collapse    * Cardiac MRI:      - Thickened pericardium greater than 40 mm      - Evidence of ventricular interdependence and septal bounce      Image Title: Figure 2      Image Description: Contrast-enhanced axial computed tomography scan showing uniformly thickened pericardium- Cardiac Catheterization Results:    * At rest:      - Right atrial pressure: 15 mm Hg      - Pulmonary artery pressure: 28/17 (mean, 21) mm Hg      - Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure: 16 mm Hg with V-wave of 20      - Cardiac output: 4.08 L/min      - Cardiac index: 1.8 L/min/m²    * During passive exercise:      - Right atrium pressure: 40 mm Hg      - Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure: 50 mm Hg      - Cardiac output: 2.38 L/min      - Cardiac index: 1.02 L/min/m²      - Pulmonary artery saturation declined from 66% to 44%",
